 Guide.webp","width":750,"height":392},"thumbnail":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/thumbnail_CCPA_Applicability_2026_Guide_a829a5ad48.webp","name":"thumbnail_CCPA Applicability_ 2026 Guide.webp","width":245,"height":128}},"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_Applicability_2026_Guide_a829a5ad48.webp","createdAt":"2025-12-05T12:41:11.989Z"},"title":"CCPA Applicability - 2026 Guide: How to Know if the Law Applies to Your Business","publish_date":"December 05 2025","last_updated":null,"last_updated_iso":null,"publish_date_iso":"2025-12-05T12:00:00.000Z","author_name":"Alex Margau","reading_time":7},{"__component":"ui.article-summary","id":1083,"title":"CCPA Applicability Guide 2026: How to Know if the Law Applies to Your Business","summary":"This CCPA applicability guide for 2026 helps businesses understand whether the California Consumer Privacy Act applies to them by breaking down key applicability thresholds. It explains how the updated $26.625M revenue threshold works, how the 100,000‑consumer data volume rule is calculated through website traffic and identifiers, and when the 50% data broker revenue rule applies. The guide also clarifies CCPA extraterritorial coverage, outlines how online tracking technologies can trigger CCPA obligations, and highlights practical next steps for organizations assessing their CCPA compliance status.\n"},{"__component":"section.markdown","id":16107,"content":"Determining **CCPA applicability** is the first and most important step in understanding your privacy obligations.\n\nWith the expanded requirements introduced under the California Privacy Rights Act (CPRA) and new inflation adjustments taking effect in 2026, the scope of who must comply has shifted. A business that was exempt last year may now fall within scope due to updated revenue thresholds or expanded definitions around “data brokerage.”\n\nThis guide explains the three legal thresholds that trigger coverage, clarifies **who must comply with CCPA** even outside California, and links to deeper resources on key data categories.\n\n\u003cbr/\u003e\n\n## **CCPA applicability checklist: Quick “Am I covered?” logic flow (2026)**\n\n*Use this quick decision sequence to assess your status:*\n\n1. **Are you a for-profit entity doing business in California?**  \n   (Yes → Continue)  \n2. **Do you meet ANY ONE of the following 3 criteria?**  \n   * **A.** Annual Global Gross Revenue \\\u003e **$26.625 million**  \n   * **B.** Buy/Sell/Share data of \\\u003e **100,000** CA consumers or households  \n   * **C.** Derive \\\u003e **50%** of annual revenue from selling/sharing data  \n3. **Result:** If you checked **A, B, or C**, the CCPA generally applies.\n\n\u003cbr/\u003e\n\n## **CCPA revenue threshold 2026 explained: Understanding the $26.6M rule**\n\nMany businesses assume the revenue threshold applies only to money made within California, but the scope is broader. Effective January 1, 2025, the original $25 million statutory threshold is adjusted for inflation. For the 2026 compliance year, your business is covered if its **annual gross revenues exceed $26,625,000**.\n\n### **Global revenue rule under CCPA: why worldwide revenue counts**\n\nThe law evaluates your full economic footprint, not just your activity within the state.\n\n* **Global scope:** This threshold typically refers to total **global gross revenue**, not revenue earned in California. For example, if your company is based in France, generates $30M worldwide, and earns only $100k from California customers, you likely meet this threshold if you are considered to be “doing business” in the state.  \n* **Inflation updates:** The CPPA updates this [monetary threshold](https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/cpi_adjustment.html) every two years based on the Consumer Price Index (CPI).\n\n**Compliance Tip:** The statute (Civil Code § 1798.140) still lists “$25,000,000,” but footnotes that the figure is updated under § 1798.199.95(d). **Use the adjusted $26.625M amount in your assessments.**\n\n\u003cbr/\u003e\n\n## **CCPA data volume threshold: does 100,000 consumers apply to your business?**\n\nThis threshold often surprises smaller companies because it focuses on **data volume, not revenue**. Your business falls under the CCPA if it annually buys, sells, or shares the personal information of **100,000 or more** California consumers, households, or devices.\n\n### **The 275 visitors rule: how website traffic triggers CCPA coverage**\n\nThis is the most common pitfall for SMBs. You do not need 100,000 customers, the law counts **unique identifiers** such as cookies, device IDs, and IP addresses.\n\n* **The calculation:**  \n  100,000 unique visitors ÷ 365 days ≈ **274 visitors per day**  \n* **The reality:**  \n  If your website averages around **275 daily visitors from California**, and you use tools such as Google Analytics, Meta Pixel, or similar trackers, you are collecting personal information from 100,000 “consumers or devices” each year.  \n* **The verdict:** If this applies, you are required to comply.\n\n**Pro Tip \\#1:** Adopting [**CCPA data minimization strategies**](https://www.clym.io/blog/ccpa-compliance-data-minimization) may help reduce your identifier volume and keep you below the threshold.\n\n**Pro Tip \\#2:** Check your Google Analytics “California” user count for the past 12 months. If “Users” exceed 100,000, you are in scope.\n\n\u003cbr/\u003e\n\n## **CCPA data broker threshold: understanding the 50% revenue rule (2026)**\n\nIf your business model relies heavily on data monetization, pay close attention to how your revenue is generated. You fall within the CCPA if **50% or more** of your annual revenue comes from selling or sharing personal information.\n\n### **Delete Act (SB 362\\) requirements for 2026: what data brokers must do**\n\nMeeting this threshold often means you qualify as a **Data Broker**, a business that knowingly collects and sells personal information from consumers with whom it does not have a direct relationship.\n\nStarting in 2026, additional Delete Act obligations apply:\n\n* **Registration:** Register with the CPPA by **January 31** each year and pay the **$6,000 registration fee**  \n* **DROP integration:** Integrate with the state’s [DROP Platform](https://privacy.ca.gov/drop/), enabling consumers to delete their information across all registered brokers via a single request\n\n\u003cbr/\u003e\n\n## **Does CCPA apply outside California? Understanding extraterritorial coverage (2026)**\n\nThe CCPA applies based on **consumer location**, not business location. A physical presence in California is not required.\n\nIf you also collect data from residents of other states, 19 additional state privacy laws are now in effect with their own applicability thresholds. See our [U.S. state privacy law comparison guide](https://www.clym.io/blog/us-privacy-law-comparison-map) for a side-by-side breakdown.\n\n### **Common scenarios**"},{"__component":"ui.ui-table","id":1459,"full_width":true,"top_align":false,"columns":[{"id":4462,"title":"Business type","rows":[{"id":26040,"content":"SaaS Platform"},{"id":26041,"content":"E-Commerce"},{"id":26042,"content":"Non-Profit"},{"id":26043,"content":"\"Hybrid\" Non-Profit"}]},{"id":4463,"title":"Scenario","rows":[{"id":26044,"content":"HQ in New York; 150,000 free-tier users logging in from CA IPs."},{"id":26045,"content":"HQ in London, UK; ships luxury goods to CA; global revenue $50M."},{"id":26046,"content":"A charity located in San Francisco."},{"id":26047,"content":"Shares brand/logo with a for-profit entity."}]},{"id":4464,"title":"Verdict","rows":[{"id":26048,"content":"Covered (Meets 100k volume threshold)"},{"id":26049,"content":"Covered (Revenue threshold + doing business in CA)"},{"id":26050,"content":"Exempt (Generally)"},{"id":26051,"content":"Covered (Common branding rule)"}]}]},{"__component":"section.markdown","id":16108,"content":"## **What data is regulated under CCPA? Full scope of covered information**\n\nOnce you confirm applicability, the next step is understanding which data categories the CCPA covers.\n\n### **CCPA personal information definition: what counts as PI?**\n\nUnder the CCPA, “Personal Information” (PI) is broad and includes:\n\n* **Identifiers:** IP addresses, cookie IDs, mobile device IDs  \n* **Internet activity:** Browsing history, search history  \n* **Inferences:** Profiles built about preferences, characteristics, or behaviors\n\n### **Selling vs. sharing under CCPA: what’s the difference?**\n\n“Selling” does not require money to change hands.\n\n* **Selling:** Exchanging PI for monetary or other valuable consideration  \n* **Sharing:** Disclosing PI for **Cross-Context Behavioral Advertising** (e.g., retargeting)\n\n*Impact:* If your website uses third-party ad pixels, you are likely “sharing” data and must provide an opt-out link.\n\n\u003cbr/\u003e\n\n## **CCPA compliance next steps: what to do if the law applies to you**\n\nCompliance is continuous, not a one-time setup. If you meet any threshold, or if your traffic averages \\~275 CA visitors per day, you should begin taking action.\n\n1. **Scan your website:** Use [Clym’s Cookie Scanner](https://www.clym.io/products/cookie-scanner) to identify every cookie and tracker contributing to your \"100,000\" count.  \n2. **Update your privacy policy:** Confirm it reflects 2026 requirements and updated thresholds.  \n3. **Start your data inventory:** Read our [CCPA compliance guide for businesses in 2026](https://www.clym.io/blog/ccpa-compliance-guide-for-businesses) to begin mapping your data flows.  \n4. **Know the risks:** Review the latest [CCPA penalties \u0026 fines](https://www.clym.io/blog/ccpa-penalties-and-fines-what-businesses-need-to-know) to understand the cost of inaction.","custom_class":"clym-md-summary"},{"__component":"block.blog-question","id":890,"title":"## **Turn applicability into an action plan**","content":"Once you know the CCPA applies to you, this checklist walks through exactly what to do next.","cta":"CCPA compliance checklist","url":"https://www.clym.io/blog/ccpa-compliance-data-minimization"},{"__component":"section.markdown","id":16109,"content":"For a complete overview of all your obligations, read our [ultimate guide on CCPA compliance](https://www.clym.io/blog/ccpa-compliance-guide-for-businesses).","custom_class":"clym-md-summary"},{"__component":"section.markdown-summary","id":2791,"html_component":"h2"},{"__component":"section.author-card","author_name":"Alex Margau","author_role":"Compliance Content Manager","author_photo":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/alex_7795ba6657.webp","published_date":null,"reading_time":0,"social_links":[],"short_bio":"**Compliance Content Manager | CIPP/E (IAPP) | CPACC (IAAP)**\n\nAlex is a Compliance Content Manager at Clym, where he researches and writes about everything related to data privacy and web accessibility compliance for businesses, helping them stay informed on their compliance needs and spreading awareness about making the web safer and more inclusive. When he's not writing about compliance, Alex has his nose in a book or is hiking in the great outdoors.","cta":[{"id":13545,"label":"Find out more about Alex","url":"/author/alex-margau","color":"primary","bold":false,"underline":false,"position":"left","disabled":false,"has_arrow":false}]},{"__component":"section.related_articles","related_articles":[{"slug":"/ccpa-compliance-guide-for-businesses","title":"CCPA Compliance: 2026 Guide for Businesses","description":"This comprehensive guide explains the definitive requirements for CCPA compliance in 2026, including the expanded rights under CPRA and the new Delete Act (SB 362). It provides a practical roadmap for managing data inventories, updating privacy notices, and handling consumer requests to keep your business audit-ready.\n","publish_date":"2025-12-01T10:00:00.000Z","categories":[{"title":"Data privacy"}],"card_media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_compliance_checklist_download_your_action_plan_today_Meta_Image_f1a43bb028.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA compliance 2026 guide for businesses blog visual"},"media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_compliance_checklist_download_your_action_plan_today_587d47a48c.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA compliance 2026 guide for businesses blog visual"}},{"slug":"/ccpa-selling-and-sharing-what-counts-as-a-sale-or-share","title":"CCPA Selling \u0026 Sharing in 2026: What Counts as a Sale or Share?","description":"Explore what selling or sharing means under the CCPA in 2026, how it applies to websites and digital tools, and what steps businesses can take to handle data responsibly.\n","publish_date":"2025-12-08T08:00:00.000Z","categories":[{"title":"Data privacy"}],"card_media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/Selling_vs_Sharing_Under_CCPA_Meta_Image_645a25080a.webp","alternativeText":"Selling vs Sharing Under CCPA blog visual"},"media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/Selling_vs_Sharing_Under_CCPA_06a2d481a6.webp","alternativeText":"Selling vs Sharing Under CCPA blog visual"}},{"slug":"/what-counts-as-sharing-personal-information-under-the-ccpa","title":"What Counts as Sharing Personal Information Under the CCPA","description":"Read our clear breakdown of what sharing personal information means under the CCPA in 2026, especially for advertising, targeting, and cross-site tracking.\n","publish_date":"2025-12-10T10:00:00.000Z","categories":[{"title":"Data privacy"}],"card_media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_Data_Sharing_Guide_Meta_Image_e06213b2a1.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA Data Sharing Guide blog visual"},"media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_Data_Sharing_Guide_b383950c11.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA Data Sharing Guide blog visual"}},{"slug":"/what-counts-as-selling-personal-information-under-the-ccpa","title":"What Counts as Selling Personal Information Under the CCPA?","description":"Read our detailed overview of what the CCPA considers selling personal information in 2026, with examples across analytics, advertising, and business partnerships.","publish_date":"2025-12-09T10:00:00.000Z","categories":[{"title":"Data privacy"}],"card_media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_Data_Sale_Guide_Meta_Image_adf2cead4b.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA Data Sale Guide blog visual"},"media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_Data_Sale_Guide_dff04c8c4e.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA Data Sale Guide blog visual"}},{"slug":"/ccpa-selling-and-sharing-examples","title":"CCPA Selling \u0026 Sharing Examples: Real-World Scenarios Businesses Face in 2026","description":"Explore practical examples of how selling and sharing happen under the CCPA in 2026, with scenarios across advertising, analytics, data enrichment, and partner integrations.\n","publish_date":"2025-12-10T12:00:00.000Z","categories":[{"title":"Data privacy"}],"card_media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_Selling_and_Sharing_2026_Scenarios_Meta_Image_db687580e8.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA Selling \u0026 Sharing 2026 Scenarios blog visual"},"media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_Selling_and_Sharing_2026_Scenarios_0ed307d438.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA Selling \u0026 Sharing 2026 Scenarios blog visual"}},{"slug":"/how-to-handle-selling-and-sharing-personal-information-under-the-ccpa","title":"How to Handle Selling and Sharing Personal Information Under the CCPA: Opt-outs, Notices, GPC, Contracts, and Interfaces","description":"A practical breakdown of how businesses can handle selling and sharing personal information under the CCPA in 2026, including opt-out requirements, GPC, and contract updates.\n","publish_date":"2025-12-11T08:00:00.000Z","categories":[{"title":"Data privacy"}],"card_media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_2026_Data_Handling_Guide_Meta_Image_ae7d10b801.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA 2026 Data Handling Guide blog visual"},"media":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_2026_Data_Handling_Guide_cfc502b639.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA 2026 Data Handling Guide blog visual"}}],"title":"Related articles"}],"og":{"id":6484,"title":"CCPA Applicability 2026: Thresholds and Rules","description":"See the exact CCPA applicability thresholds for 2026, revenue, consumer, and data-sale criteria, so you know in minutes if the law applies to you.","image":{"id":2417,"name":"CCPA Applicability_ 2026 Guide- Meta Image.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA Applicability 2026 Guide blog visual","width":588,"height":288,"formats":{"small":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/small_CCPA_Applicability_2026_Guide_Meta_Image_08da748887.webp","name":"small_CCPA Applicability_ 2026 Guide- Meta Image.webp","width":500,"height":245},"thumbnail":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/thumbnail_CCPA_Applicability_2026_Guide_Meta_Image_08da748887.webp","name":"thumbnail_CCPA Applicability_ 2026 Guide- Meta Image.webp","width":245,"height":120}},"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/CCPA_Applicability_2026_Guide_Meta_Image_08da748887.webp","createdAt":"2025-12-05T12:41:11.142Z"}},"seo":{"id":914,"seo_title":"CCPA Applicability 2026: Thresholds and Rules","seo_description":"See the exact CCPA applicability thresholds for 2026, revenue, consumer, and data-sale criteria, so you know in minutes if the law applies to you.","no_index":false,"no_follow":false},"className":"blog-page","page":{"id":2997,"title":"CCPA Applicability - 2026 Guide: How to Know if the Law Applies to Your Business","description":"Take a closer look at how the CCPA may apply to your business in 2026 with this straightforward threshold guide. Explore examples covering revenue, data volume, and data-broker status.","slug":"/ccpa-applicability-guide","publish_date":"2025-12-05T12:00:00.000Z","createdAt":"2025-12-05T12:29:20.745Z","locale":"en","reading_time":7,"media":{"id":2418,"name":"CCPA Applicability_ 2026 Guide.webp","alternativeText":"CCPA Applicability 2026 Guide blog visual","width":1200,"height":628,"formats":{"large":{"url":"https://cdn-www.clym.io/web/l